# sh2fleet.env — the environment every worker unit needs.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (2026-09-27). A systemd --user unit does NOT inherit the
# interactive shell's PATH. It gets systemd's own minimal default, and on this
# box that default is:
#
#   /root/.nix-profile/bin:/nix/var/nix/profiles/default/bin:/usr/local/sbin:
#   /usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/games:/usr/local/games:
#   /snap/bin
#
# — which does NOT contain /usr/local/cargo/bin, so `cargo` is simply not
# found. Every worker that shells out to cargo therefore failed INSTANTLY with
# "cargo: command not found" on stderr and no compiler output at all.
#
# The symptom was thoroughly misleading: the core worker logged
#
#   core_verify: isolated core build FAILED — skipping iteration
#
# with not one line of compiler output, and separately
#
#   lib tests produced NO summary (hung or crashed — NOT a pass)
#
# so the diagnosis pointed at a broken build and then at a hanging TEST. Both
# were a missing PATH. This is the same class of drift as the original
# `User=ai` / `/nvme/ai/sh2loop` unit bug: a checked-in unit that describes an
# environment nobody runs in, so it fails for a reason the log never names.
#
# It is a SYSTEMD gap, not a build-lock gap, and it cannot be fixed inside
# build-lock.sh or core_verify.sh: those run *after* the shell has already
# failed to resolve `cargo`.
#
# CARGO/RUSTUP: cargo lives in /usr/local/cargo/bin and rustup's toolchains in
# /usr/local/rustup, so both are on PATH here. Keep these in sync with whatever
# `command -v cargo` reports on the box.

PATH=/usr/local/cargo/bin:/root/.cargo/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/games:/usr/local/games:/snap/bin
CARGO_HOME=/usr/local/cargo
RUSTUP_HOME=/usr/local/rustup

# ── MODEL ROTATION: route every LLM worker through ai_invoke.py ─────────
#
# WHY (2026-09-28). Both loops defaulted to invoking `pir` DIRECTLY, which
# broke three things at once:
#
#   1. No prompt delivery. `pir` takes its prompt as a POSITIONAL argument
#      and ignores stdin, so the loops' prompt never reached the model at
#      all. The estree log shows the result: hundreds of iterations of
#      "Invoking pir ... (pi finished)" with no output and no edits.
#   2. No quota fallback. The daily-free fallback to
#      ollama-cloud/deepseek-v4.1-flash lives in harness/ai_invoke.py, which
#      the loops only call when WORKER_ROTATE_WORKER is set. Unset, both
#      loops hit poolside/laguna-s-2.1:free directly and died on
#      "HTTP 429: free-models-per-day" with nothing to catch it.
#   3. No rotation. MODEL_ROTATION.md documents rust/c as group A and
#      perl/estree as group B, swapping every four hours. With the variable
#      unset, all four workers sat on the FREE model at once, sharing one
#      1000/day quota — which is precisely how it got exhausted.
#
# Setting the variable routes the AI call through ai_invoke.py, which
# selects the phase's model, applies the quota fallback, records token
# telemetry, and invokes `cmd -p` — a CLI that genuinely reads the prompt
# from stdin (verified live: a piped prompt returns a result frame).
#
# One value per unit, set in the unit's own Environment= line: this file is
# shared, and the per-worker identity comes from the unit, not from here.
#
# To run a loop OUTSIDE the rotation (e.g. to debug), drop the unit's
# WORKER_ROTATE_WORKER= line; the loops then fall back to calling pir
# directly, which is the pre-2026-09-28 behaviour.
